WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
Film Courage: Do you think that most&nbsp;
Hollywood filmmakers started out as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.800
quote-unquote working-class&nbsp;
filmmakers? Or do you think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.920
there was something that was&nbsp;
different in their journey?

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.600
Evan Kidd, Filmmaker/Podcaster: Yeah it's hard to&nbsp;
say. I’m certainly not a Hollywood filmmaker so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:23.040
it's hard to know exactly but from what I’ve&nbsp;
gathered over the years watching many interviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:30.400
going to film festivals, meeting some filmmakers&nbsp;
that I love, I’ve understood there's two paths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.640
to do it. There's the hardest path which&nbsp;
is almost like playing the lottery which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:42.960
is essentially making sure you are in the right&nbsp;
place at the right time with the right people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:49.040
and you have a bunch of connections and you have&nbsp;
a bunch of people around you who know how to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.960
this happen and you're also privileged enough&nbsp;
to have all those connections fall into place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:03.520
so that's tough because 99 of people can't&nbsp;
quite get there but I think a lot of times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.600
the other route which is kind of I guess the&nbsp;
way i'd like to go which is maybe the the road&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.680
less traveled which I feel like is just making&nbsp;
many smaller films over a period of time and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:22.160
trying to make an impact with that and seeing&nbsp;
what can happen and seeing what is available to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.440
you with the resources you have around you so&nbsp;
I would certainly kind of look at it that way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.960
but it is certainly interesting because I think&nbsp;
a lot of times people think the hollywood way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.360
is the only way and I think with with&nbsp;
time people are seeing that it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:45.120
well since you've done music videos you've&nbsp;
probably seen bands or artists whatever in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.160
the music industry that probably maybe they&nbsp;
didn't set out to be big names but then somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.680
their career took them that way I wonder if that's&nbsp;
just the way it happens it's just people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.080
just really have a desire to tell something&nbsp;
whether it's through music or film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:06.320
and then ends up taking them there yeah I&nbsp;
got to work with a rapper out of baton rouge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.760
named caleb brown who recently got signed to&nbsp;
rostr records a couple years back and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.680
Mac Miller and Wiz Khalifa's label and I kind of&nbsp;
got a little peek behind the curtain of how a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.760
label runs and it was really interesting because&nbsp;
they in their instance were really kind and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:31.360
let him do a lot of his own stuff and they got to&nbsp;
really trust their artist which I think is rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.320
the little bit of understanding I could gather&nbsp;
and that's certainly different because it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.680
music industry was that a lot of labels don't&nbsp;
run that way and so it was it was interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:47.120
to kind of get a glimpse of that and then kind of&nbsp;
apply it to what I do which is the film industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.120
which also does not always run that&nbsp;
way because I think a lot of times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.960
if you do get into the big league so to speak you&nbsp;
sometimes have to compromise some of your morals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.160
or your values or the types of films you want to&nbsp;
make you may have to make that rent check so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.120
just do something that kind of takes you off the&nbsp;
path that you're hoping to go and sometimes you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:13.440
end up down that path for a while and you kind of&nbsp;
forget the original path and so I always try to as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.520
best as possible stay connected to the the source&nbsp;
message of what resonates with me as an artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.920
and it seems like so many artists&nbsp;
whatever the genre start out as more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:32.720
the introverted kid in their bedroom doodling&nbsp;
or creating music or doing a little film set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:37.280
and they're not really looking to be part&nbsp;
of that big group they just love doing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.800
and then if they're really good it somehow takes&nbsp;
them out into the big group and then they're like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.280
wait a minute I don't know if I want to be here I&nbsp;
like doing it on my own that's where I feel safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.280
yeah yeah it's certainly really interesting&nbsp;
because I i kind of feel that on a personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:04:01.360
level for myself like I love going to big crews&nbsp;
and when I was doing music videos like we we did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:07.600
a giant crew for one and it was weird because&nbsp;
it was like I don't touch that camera like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:12.880
that's his job is like it would be weird if I&nbsp;
went over and touched that camera and like for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.680
coming from north carolina like sometimes we&nbsp;
would pass the camera around to like see different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.800
people's ideas and like that's just not the way&nbsp;
it works on a professional set and that's okay and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.560
that's by design and certainly like my films now&nbsp;
I don't do that but it's just interesting kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.160
like life happens fast but sometimes you blink&nbsp;
and you find yourself on those bigger sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:40.640
and it's certainly a very cool experience but&nbsp;
then sometimes people say oh once i'm there i'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:46.720
never going back one of the things I did while I&nbsp;
was making that music video was I was making that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:53.120
couple hundred dollar tv show in louisiana home&nbsp;
remedy and two days after I did that big shoot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.680 --> 00:05:00.720
I drove a couple hours down across Louisiana for&nbsp;
a shoot on the tv show and it was just me and two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:07.280
people with a canon 60. and so it was like I&nbsp;
just wanted to make that story and that wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:13.040
the story that was going to pay my bills the music&nbsp;
video was but it was something to me that I didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:18.080
feel like just because I got here didn't mean I&nbsp;
couldn't go back to my roots when I wanted to so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.320
we heard you almost didn't go to film school Evan&nbsp;
is that true I thought about a couple different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.800
options I ended up going to film school because&nbsp;
I loved the professors who I met I went to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:37.040
very small film school that's within a larger&nbsp;
art department at East Carolina University in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:43.360
North Carolina and I loved the the faculty who&nbsp;
were there because they they truly were very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:50.640
realistic with me about the odds of filmmaking&nbsp;
which you would think would scare people off but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:56.960
to me actually I appreciated the the earnestness&nbsp;
and the honesty and the sincerity that they said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:02.080
there they basically said we'll teach you how to&nbsp;
make a film but it's not going to guarantee that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.880
you're going to go to be Spielberg and I had just&nbsp;
gone through a bunch of college tours at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.800
with people saying hey do you want to be the next&nbsp;
Spielberg do you want to be the next this and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:17.760
and it felt gimmicky and you could kind of feel&nbsp;
a little bit of the pull and I mean of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.840
someone's going to be the next whatever&nbsp;
right that's just how the world works but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.520
the odds are that it's probably not going to be&nbsp;
you so instead of billing it as do you want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:34.720
the next insert famous person here it was built to&nbsp;
me as do you want to know how to do this to almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:40.880
like learning a craft like welding or something&nbsp;
like that and to me that was more interesting so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.760
they they pitched that and they also pitched like&nbsp;
more practical ways to use filmmaking shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:50.960
corporate videos music videos weddings things&nbsp;
like that like I think a lot of times film schools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.920
try to like shove that part under the rug a little&nbsp;
bit and they say well that's not glamorous we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:01.360
only going to teach you the the cool stuff but&nbsp;
but they kind of opened the whole toolbox and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.320
I certainly appreciated that and I think i've&nbsp;
taken a little bit of that with me along the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:14.560
it seems like so much of anything that's I mean&nbsp;
did you watch the last dance yeah so if you if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:20.640
look at some of it it's incredibly glamorous and&nbsp;
you see but then the the viewer was allowed to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.720
behind the scenes to some parts that were very&nbsp;
very painful and then some parts that weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.640
that glamorous but we don't really think about&nbsp;
that so I think that it seems like for a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:35.520
professions like these the creme de la creme we&nbsp;
don't really see that a lot of it isn't glamorous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:42.880
and a lot of it's tedious and so I think it's&nbsp;
great that your professor or professors were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:48.400
a little more realistic with you and yeah&nbsp;
didn't try to make it seem because I think then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:54.480
people get blindsided and then yeah they&nbsp;
have dashed expectations they do yeah and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:59.200
I think that's why there's a lot of burnout&nbsp;
in the film industry and that's why people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.800
turn to things and there's a lot of mental health&nbsp;
issues people don't talk about and there's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:11.040
a lot of things that I think don't need to be&nbsp;
there if we don't set the expectation at Steven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:18.080
Spielberg if we maybe set the expectation at&nbsp;
you're learning a craft you're learning how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:22.720
do this so you can tell the stories you want&nbsp;
to tell certainly doesn't mean anyone's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:29.040
to watch but I think it's a lot of times being&nbsp;
realistic with it and also saying you can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:34.560
these stories out of your head and put them out&nbsp;
and hopefully with time people will see that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:40.320
people will take notice and and I appreciate&nbsp;
the like you said the earnestness of that.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:54.302
Question For The Viewers:

00:08:54.302 --> 00:08:57.040
Which Hollywood director&nbsp;
has the best success story?

